1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,177
Two hundred and seventy five hours.
That's the longest played review on this game's demo. Not the game. The demo.

2
00:00:07,427 --> 00:00:15,022
Half the people who reviewed it had already put in ten hours.
It's free, it's five hundred megabytes, and one person made all of it.

3
00:00:18,872 --> 00:00:28,087
Casualties: Unknown is a two-D cave survival game about being dropped down a hole to fetch cargo.
Mostly, it's about the ways a body stops working.

4
00:00:28,337 --> 00:00:38,309
Six thousand eight hundred reviews. Ninety six percent positive.
Steam calls that Overwhelmingly Positive, and most finished games never get there.

5
00:00:38,559 --> 00:00:49,184
The developer is one person in Poland who goes by Orsoniks. Before Steam this lived on itch dot io,
where it still sits at four point nine out of five from twelve hundred ratings.

6
00:00:49,434 --> 00:00:54,103
And on the store page, in plain text:
no generative AI was used.

7
00:00:54,353 --> 00:01:02,052
So how does one person, with no team and no AI,
keep people in a free demo for ten hours?

8
00:01:02,302 --> 00:01:09,296
Steam publishes a file list for every demo,
and you can read it without installing anything. That's where I started.

9
00:01:09,546 --> 00:01:17,559
Five hundred and seventeen megabytes. A hundred and seventy one files.
The last demo I took apart was three point seven gigabytes.

10
00:01:17,809 --> 00:01:22,348
It's Unity,
and it's the universal render pipeline in its two-D mode.

11
00:01:22,598 --> 00:01:30,376
That's the boring choice,
and it's why the minimum spec on the store page is a laptop processor and five hundred megabytes of disk.

12
00:01:30,626 --> 00:01:33,911
Then I opened the managed folder,
and this is where I stopped.

13
00:01:34,161 --> 00:01:45,335
The entire game — every system, every item, every wound — is one file.
Assembly dash C sharp dot D L L. Eight hundred and fifty eight kilobytes.

14
00:01:45,585 --> 00:01:54,199
The Unity runtime sitting next to it, the part Unity wrote, is thirty megabytes.
The engine is thirty five times bigger than the game it runs.

15
00:01:54,449 --> 00:02:00,607
And there are two scenes in the build. Two.
One is eighty kilobytes, the other is three hundred and thirty.

16
00:02:00,857 --> 00:02:10,307
So where's the game? None of it is laid out by hand.
It's generated, and it runs off data files.

17
00:02:10,557 --> 00:02:16,349
Next I went looking for the shopping list.
The third party packages every Unity project leans on.

18
00:02:16,599 --> 00:02:22,156
There is one. Discord rich presence,
so your friends can see which layer you died on.

19
00:02:22,406 --> 00:02:32,038
No audio middleware. No inspector plugin. No character controller.
No save framework. The game I took apart last time had more than twenty of those.

20
00:02:32,288 --> 00:02:41,503
There's a real trade here, and it's worth saying out loud.
Packages get you shipped. Writing it yourself gets you something nobody else has.

21
00:02:41,753 --> 00:02:45,456
There is one more thing in that
file list I did not expect to find.

22
00:02:45,706 --> 00:02:54,320
A folder called Lang. English, three hundred and forty eight kilobytes of text.
Simplified Chinese, three hundred and thirty five.

23
00:02:54,570 --> 00:03:03,941
The Steam page says this game supports English only. The Chinese is in the build anyway,
because the translations are made by the community and the developer just ships them.

24
00:03:04,191 --> 00:03:11,708
You can see it land. Of the last eight hundred reviews,
four hundred and seventy six are English. A hundred and seventy five are Russian.

25
00:03:11,958 --> 00:03:19,605
There's also a folder called custom music, with a read me inside.
You can drop your own tracks into the game and it will play them.

26
00:03:19,855 --> 00:03:25,047
None of that comes free with Unity.
Somebody sat down and decided each one.

27
00:03:25,297 --> 00:03:29,183
So what do people say about it?

28
00:03:29,433 --> 00:03:37,994
The praise is about depth and atmosphere. The complaints are almost entirely about the interface,
and about losing an eight hour run to a crash.

29
00:03:38,244 --> 00:03:45,238
And the split is the widest I've measured.
People who recommended it had played eleven hours. People who didn't, one.

30
00:03:45,488 --> 00:03:47,832
Here's the whole teardown on one card.

31
00:03:48,082 --> 00:03:57,218
Unity two-D. One developer. Years on itch dot io before it ever reached Steam.
Almost no third party code at all.

32
00:03:57,468 --> 00:04:04,279
And the trick worth stealing: eight hundred and fifty eight kilobytes of systems,
instead of gigabytes of content.

33
00:04:04,529 --> 00:04:08,702
Worth a dip. It's free,
and it will cost you an evening. Or ten.

34
00:04:08,952 --> 00:04:19,838
If you're one or two people, this is the counter argument to the last episode.
That game bought every hard system and shipped in months. This one wrote them and took years.

35
00:04:20,088 --> 00:04:29,485
Both of those work. Just know which one you're doing.
Next time, another demo, another file list. This is Demo Dip.
